#
# prima-materia — the Anatolian practitioner rituals, taken off the register
#
# Taken from traditions/registry-west-asia.ttl on the testability-driven
# entrance of SPEC 14, and chosen over the other two registrations at
# pm:contactRouteDisputed for one reason: this corpus writes its rituals down
# in the first person of the practitioner who dictated them, and a text that
# says I act as follows is the place where a stated condition of failure would
# have to stand if a transmission carries one at all. The standing finding at
# prima-materia#640 is that the holding had collected twelve announced signs of
# success against no condition of failure.
#
# The corpus answered that in the sharpest form the holding has yet carried.
# One of the five works opened names the negative outcome of its own
# announced sign and says, in the practitioner's first person, what follows
# from it: nothing follows, the ritual is performed again. That is a
# Misslingensbedingung with its remedy attached, written down by the operator,
# and it is the first witness outside the Chinese material for the claim in
# generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl.
#
# A second of the five fixes counts of the same kind and supplies no such
# provision anywhere in its preserved end. The two stand in one tradition, and
# the protocol that follows from the pair is in
# examinations/the-count-that-does-not-fall-due.ttl.
#
# The three decisions SPEC 14 requires before the first node:
#
# The contact route stays at pm:contactRouteDisputed and the reading did not
# move it; what it added is recorded at the instance. The recension and the
# edition are named in every source and are, for four of the five works, the
# critical editions of the Mainz Beschwörungsrituale corpus, cited by CTH
# number, exemplar and tablet locus. And the number of citable places: the
# corpus is numerous, the works are short, five were opened in full at the
# wording and twenty-two places are entered from them.
#
# The works of this corpus have no title. Each tablet designates itself in its
# colophon as the word of the practitioner who dictated it, and the catalogue
# and every edition designate them the same way. The pmw: identifiers are
# therefore the practitioner's name in the leading transliteration of the
# edition used, and nothing else; that rule is stated here so that a second
# run produces the same identifier.
#

pmt:HittiteRitual a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Hittite and Anatolian ritual"@en, "Hethitisch-anatolisches Ritual"@de ;
    skos:definition "Anatolian purification and substitution rituals transmitted under the names of the practitioners who dictated them, most of them women."@en ;
    skos:note "The registration's clause that most of the practitioners are women held for four of the five works read, and the fifth is the case that shows what the clause rests on. The two manuscripts of the ritual of Ḫantitaššu disagree about it inside a single line: the exemplar made for general use writes the female determinative before the name and then the sign for man after it, while the exemplar the editors identify as made for the king writes the female determinative and then the sign for woman. The editors of the critical edition state the divergence. The clause is therefore kept as the registration wrote it, and the one case against it is entered at pmc:HittKolophonHantitassuDisputatum rather than smoothed away."@en ;
    skos:note "What the reading added to the contact route, which stays disputed. The practitioners are named in the texts themselves as foreign to the capital, and in two directions at once: Maštigga is the woman of Kizzuwatna in her incipit and the woman of Kummanni in her colophon, which is the cult centre of that same south-eastern land; Paškuwatti is the woman of Arzawa who dwells in Parašša, and Uḫḫamuwa is the man of Arzawa, which lies to the west. Ḫantitaššu is of Ḫurma. So the corpus records its own rituals as imported from both ends of Anatolia, and that is a datum for the dispute rather than a resolution of it: it establishes that the texts present themselves as received, and leaves open whether the technique came with the name. The position that would settle it asks of each proposed Hurrian or Mesopotamian element whether it is specific, exceptional and co-occurrent with others; that position was reached in the landscape stage of this run and not read at the wording, so it is not asserted here."@en ;
    skos:note "A second route question the reading opened and did not close, and it bears on the strand that was run beside this one. The Ugaritic expiation text RS 1.002 names the Hittite among the parties whose statement may have caused the sin it expiates. That establishes contact between the two corpora and nothing about transmission of technique; but it is enough that a convergence asserted between pmt:HittiteRitual and pmt:UgariticRitual may not be entered as pm:independentAttestation without a pm:independenceGround that addresses it. See traditions/ugaritic-ritual.ttl."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The CTH ritual corpus, including the rituals of Tunnawiya (CTH 409), Maštigga (CTH 404), Ḫantitaššu (CTH 395), Paškuwatti (CTH 406), Uḫḫamuwa (CTH 410), Ambazzi (CTH 391) and Anniwiyani (CTH 393)" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:Tunnawiya, pmw:Mastigga, pmw:Hantitassu, pmw:Paskuwatti, pmw:Uhhamuwa, pmw:Ambazzi, pmw:Anniwiyani ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDisputed ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 1-4 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#

pmw:Tunnawiya a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of Tunnawiya"@en, "Das Ritual der Tunnawiya"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the only edition that carries transliteration and translation together: A. Goetze, interpreted in cooperation with E. H. Sturtevant, The Hittite Ritual of Tunnawi, American Oriental Series 14, New Haven: American Oriental Society 1938, with its text, translation, commentary and glossary read through, and the title page read in the full text and not in the catalogue entry. The tablet is the join the edition itself states, Bo 2062 (KUB 7.53) with Bo 2589 (KUB 12.58), which in the modern form is KUB 7.53+."@en ;
    skos:note "This work carries a title of its own, which the others do not, and it is in the colophon: the first tablet of the ritual of uncleanness and of the ritual of the river, this being the one of Tunnawiya the Old Woman, complete, written by Pikku. The identifier still follows the rule stated at the instance, because the rule has to produce one identifier for a corpus in which four of five colophons give only the name."@en ;
    skos:note "A second philological treatment of the same tablet is reachable and was used as the check on the 1938 transliteration: the independent transliteration with interlinear glossing in the Thesaurus Linguarum Hethaeorum digitalis, hethiter.net/: TLHdig KUB 7.53+ (2021-12-31). It is not a second translation, and no second translation of this work is reachable; the only one named in the current bibliography is in print. What was read at the wording is therefore one translation beside the original, and that is less than the five points of SPEC 15 require. See prima-materia#691."@en ;
    skos:note "The script dating of this composition is late. Of the fragments the concordance assigns to CTH 409 the great majority are catalogued as New Hittite copies, with a single Middle Hittite fragment; the tablet read here is in the late group. The composition is therefore attested essentially only in copies made long after the practitioner it names."@en .

pmw:Mastigga a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of Maštigga"@en, "Das Ritual der Maštigga"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition of the second version, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II exemplar A, textus 2012-05-07, whose main manuscript is KBo 2.3 joined with KBo 45.191 and IBoT 4.13 and collated against KUB 12.34, KUB 15.39, KUB 12.59, IBoT 2.109, IBoT 4.12 and KUB 41.12. Forty-four paragraphs, read in order."@en ;
    skos:note "Two translations of this same version were read beside the original and they disagree at the place this file was opened for. English: A. Goetze, Ritual against Domestic Quarrel, in J. B. Pritchard (ed.), Ancient Near Eastern Texts Relating to the Old Testament, 3rd ed. 1969, pp. 350-351, which names KBo 2.3 and the same duplicates as its text. German: F. Hrozný, Hethitische Keilschrifttexte aus Boghazköi. In Umschrift, mit Übersetzung und Kommentar, Boghazköi-Studien 3, Leipzig: J. C. Hinrichs 1919, pp. 60-89, the editio princeps of this tablet, with transliteration and translation. The disagreement is at pmc:HittKisibApiyakkuDisputatum."@en ;
    skos:note "The composition has three versions in the catalogue, CTH 404.1.I, .II and .III, and they are not duplicates. The first is edited from KBo 39.8 and is catalogued as Middle Hittite in script; the second, read here, is the one the two reachable translations treat. A redactional history of the group was published in 2004 and is in print and was not read. Which version is prior is therefore not asserted anywhere in this file."@en .

pmw:Hantitassu a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of Ḫantitaššu"@en, "Das Ritual der Ḫantitaššu"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition hethiter.net/: CTH 395.1, textus 2015-06-16, both exemplars: A is KBo 11.14, a four-column tablet whose reverse fourth column is preserved with its colophon, and B is KUB 43.57 with KBo 18.174. The second version of the same ritual, hethiter.net/: CTH 395.2.1, whose single manuscript is KUB 57.79, was read in its preserved fourth column for the same places."@en ;
    skos:note "No translation of this work is reachable. The two editions that carry one, of 1996 and of 2016, are in print. What was read beside the original is the editors' own German paragraph-by-paragraph summary of contents printed with the edition, which is a check on the structure and not a translation of the wording. Every place entered from this work therefore rests on the original read without a translation beside it, and that is stated at each node that needs it. See prima-materia#691."@en ;
    skos:note "The editors state two things about the two exemplars that the nodes below depend on. A is a general version and B was made for the king. And in the colophon A designates Ḫantitaššu as a man while B designates her as a woman."@en .

pmw:Paskuwatti a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of Paškuwatti"@en, "Das Ritual der Paškuwatti"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition hethiter.net/: CTH 406 exemplar A, textus 2012-05-07 with the introduction of 2017-01-12, whose manuscripts are KUB 7.8 joined with KUB 9.27 indirectly and with KUB 7.5. The editors state that the indirect join was established by collation of the originals in 2009 and that their line numbering therefore departs from the editio princeps of 1987; the loci cited in this file are theirs."@en ;
    skos:note "The name is the one this file had to correct. The reachable English translation, A. Goetze in Pritchard (ed.), ANET, 3rd ed. 1969, pp. 349-350, reads the practitioner's name as Pissuwattis; the critical edition and the editio princeps read fPaskuwatti. The identifier follows the leading transliteration of the edition used, as the rule at the instance requires, and the earlier form is recorded here so that a reader coming from the 1969 translation finds the node."@en ;
    skos:note "No colophon is preserved. The name is taken from the incipit, where the text names itself as the word of Paškuwatti, woman of Arzawa, who dwells in Parašša."@en ;
    skos:note "Script dating: the concordance catalogues the fragments of this composition as New Hittite."@en .

pmw:Uhhamuwa a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of Uḫḫamuwa"@en, "Das Ritual des Uḫḫamuwa"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened in full at the wording on 2026-10-06 in the critical edition hethiter.net/: CTH 410, textus 2013-12-09, exemplar A, which is HT 1 (BM 108548), where the ritual runs on the obverse second column from line 17 to line 47 and is, as the editors state, completely preserved. Exemplar B, KUB 9.31, was downloaded and its divergences were not collated. A second translation was read beside the original: A. Goetze, Ritual against Pestilence, in Pritchard (ed.), ANET, 3rd ed. 1969, pp. 347-348."@en ;
    skos:note "The editors note that all four witnesses are four-column collective tablets carrying three rituals each, those of Zarpiya, of Uḫḫamuwa and of Ašḫella. The work is therefore not a tablet but a section of one, and the places entered from it are cited as such."@en .

pmw:Ambazzi a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of Ambazzi"@en, "Das Ritual der Ambazzi"@de ;
    skos:note "Not opened. The critical edition of CTH 391.1 is reachable as a transliteration and was downloaded in this run and not read; no translation of it is reachable, and the monograph that is the standard treatment of this ritual tradition is in print. This work stands here as the warranted next place in this corpus, and queries/unopened-works.rq lists it."@en .

pmw:Anniwiyani a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of Anniwiyani"@en, "Das Ritual der Anniwiyani"@de ;
    skos:note "Not opened. Two treatments are reachable: the critical edition of CTH 393 as a transliteration, and the autograph with transliteration, translation, commentary and glossary in E. H. Sturtevant and G. Bechtel, A Hittite Chrestomathy, Philadelphia: Linguistic Society of America 1935, pp. 100-126. It is therefore the cheapest of the unopened works of this corpus and the one with the best apparatus; it was left for the reason that this run's lease was five works and not seven. queries/unopened-works.rq lists it."@en .

#
# Tunnawiya, CTH 409: the ritual of uncleanness, which the tablet also calls
# the ritual of the river
#

pmc:HittPapratar a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Uncleanness as a condition a person is placed in"@en, "Unreinheit als Zustand, in den ein Mensch gesetzt ist"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "papratar"@en, "paprannaš"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition the ritual of the river is performed against: a person, man or woman, is placed in some uncleanness, or someone else has named him or her for uncleanness, or a woman's children keep dying, or they are born before term, or the sexual parts of a man or a woman are disabled in consequence of a formula of uncleanness."@en ;
    skos:note "Five indications in one conditional sentence, and they are of two kinds. Three are states a second person could observe: children that keep dying, children born before term, parts of the body disabled. The other two are not. That a person is placed in uncleanness is the diagnosis, not the sign of it; and that someone else has named him for uncleanness makes a third party's accusation an indication in its own right, with nothing in the text by which the accusation could be checked. A transmission whose entry conditions include an accusation can be entered without a case ever having been observed, and the text supplies no step that would exclude it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 1-6 (A. Goetze with E. H. Sturtevant, The Hittite Ritual of Tunnawi, American Oriental Series 14, New Haven 1938, pp. 4-5)" .

pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of uncleanness, called the ritual of the river"@en, "Das Unreinheitsritual, Flussritual genannt"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "SISKUR.SISKUR paprannaš"@en ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the Old Woman performs over the person who is experiencing uncleanness: black animals and black vessels are assembled and the patient is dressed in black and stops the ears with black wool; at nightfall the Old Woman goes to the river bank to announce the rite to its genius and takes clay from the bank and from a spring; a tent of reeds stands ready beside the river; at dawn the patient comes to the tent, and the Old Woman lifts the black sheep, the piglet, the puppy, the clay tongues, the two figures and the other prepared objects over the patient in turn, pronouncing the charm belonging to each; the figures of wax and of mutton tallow are lifted last; the clay of the bank is handed over for the cleansing of the twelve parts of the body."@en ;
    skos:note "The sequence of charms is not in this tablet. After the clay oxen the text says that the respective charms are already put down in a tablet, and that tablet is not this one. What is recoverable here is therefore the order of acts and not the words said over most of them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 7-II 20 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 4-13)" .

pmc:HittParkunuwarUzuUr a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve parts of the body purified"@en, "Die zwölf Körperteile gereinigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The attainment the rite claims for the person it is performed on: the twelve parts of the body are cleansed and purified of evil uncleanness, of sorcery and of a third named affliction, and the generative faculty is re-established, so that the house is filled with sons and daughters, grandchildren and great-grandchildren."@en ;
    skos:note "The twelve parts are counted and never listed. The number occurs four times in the tablet, in the charge to the genius of the river bank, in the Old Woman's address over the patient, in the address to the Sun-god and in the closing formula; nowhere is there an enumeration of which twelve. A claim with a count and no list cannot come out short."@en ;
    skos:note "The closing formula is partly broken and the restoration is the editors' of 1938. What stands in the preserved text is that the twelve parts have been purified; that the generative faculty was re-established is supplied by them from the context. The node is entered with the restoration named because the whole point of the tablet's opening is procreation, and the restoration is not what carries the capacity: the fruit-tree address earlier in the same column asks in the preserved text that the patient be covered like the tree and fill the house with grandchildren and great-grandchildren."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Rs. III 32 and Rs. IV 8-13, 34-38 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 7, 23-25)" .

pmc:HittTunnawiyaYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The ritual of the river is said to purify the twelve parts and restore procreation"@en, "Das Flussritual soll die zwölf Teile reinigen und die Fortpflanzung wiederherstellen"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HittParkunuwarUzuUr ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 1-9 with Rs. IV 34-38 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 4-5, 25)" .

pmc:HittTentSitePresupposed a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The tent stands where there is no cultivation and the plough does not come"@en, "Das Zelt steht, wo kein Anbau ist und der Pflug nicht hinkommt"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HittTentSite ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 39-42 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 7-9)" ;
    skos:note "The text puts the condition as a question and answers it: where do they make the tent — where there is no cultivation near by and the plough does not come, there the tent is made. It states no consequence of making it elsewhere, so the strength is prescribed order and not stated necessity. What the condition is about is not said either; the rite handles uncleanness by moving it into clay, animals and figures that are afterwards disposed of, and an uncultivated place is where such things can be left."@en .

pmc:HittTentSite a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The uncultivated place beside the river"@en, "Der unbebaute Ort am Fluss"@de ;
    skos:definition "The site at which the reed tent for the rite is erected: beside the river, where no cultivation is near and the plough does not come."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 40-42 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, p. 9)" .

pmc:HittRiverBankAnnouncement a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "The genius of the river bank is told before the clay is taken"@en, "Dem Genius des Flussufers wird angesagt, bevor der Lehm genommen wird"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HittSiskurPaprannas ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:HittRiverBankGenius ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 24-33 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 5-7)" ;
    skos:note "The Old Woman goes to the river bank at nightfall for the express purpose of announcing the rite, breaks bread, pours wine and asks the genius to take the clay into its hand and cleanse the patient with it, and only then takes the clay. The material of the rite is thus obtained by request and not simply dug; no consequence of taking it without the request is stated."@en .

pmc:HittRiverBankGenius a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The genius of the river bank"@en, "Der Genius des Flussufers"@de ;
    skos:definition "The addressee at the bank, to whom the rite is announced with bread, tallow cake, porridge and a libation of wine before the clay is taken, and who is asked to take the clay in hand and purify the twelve parts of the patient's body with it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Tunnawiya ;
    dcterms:source "Ritual of Tunnawiya, CTH 409, KUB 7.53+KUB 12.58 Vs. I 26-32 (Goetze–Sturtevant, AOS 14, 1938, pp. 5-7)" .

#
# Maštigga, CTH 404.1.II: the quarrel, the substitutes, and the seal that is to
# be opened at an event that does not occur
#

pmc:HittHalluwai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The quarrel between kin that the rite is performed against"@en, "Der Streit zwischen Verwandten, gegen den der Ritus gilt"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "ḫalluwai-"@en ;
    skos:definition "The condition named in the incipit and repeated in the colophon: a father and a son, or a husband and his wife, or a brother and a sister are to quarrel, and the practitioner sets them together again."@en ;
    skos:note "Three pairs and no others, each one a pair within a household, and the same three in the same order at both ends of the tablet. The rite is performed on two persons at once throughout — the plural of the ritual patron is used of them in every paragraph — so the party treated is the quarrel and not either quarreller."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 1-4 and Rs. IV 14-18 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" .

pmp:HittTaksanTittanumar a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Setting the quarrelling pair together again"@en, "Das Wiederzusammensetzen des streitenden Paares"@de ;
    skos:definition "The procedure performed over both quarrellers together: tongues of wax, of dough and of clay, hands of dough and figures of dough are waved over them and thrown into the hearth; salt, tallow and tissue are flattened by them with the left hand; water and dough are waved over them and they are declared clean of mouth and tongue; a white sheep, a black sheep and a piglet are presented as substitutes and the pair spit into the mouth of each; a kneading-bowl of clay with black cumin, and a second with oil and a severed thread of blue wool, are waved over them; a ḫupuwai-vessel filled with wine, oil, honey, figs, raisins, tissue, salt and tallow is poured into the hearth and broken; a further sheep is presented to the Sun-god as the scapegoat; seven stone pillars set between two fires are overturned by the pair with their feet and thrown into the fire; their fine garments and the vessel from their heads are taken off them and broken; their limbs are rubbed down with the Sun's hay; and finally they pour salted water over their heads and rinse their hands and eyes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 5-Rs. IV 6 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" .

pmc:HittMastiggaYielding a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The rite is said to cut off the words of the quarrel from the pair"@en, "Der Ritus soll die Worte des Streits vom Paar abschneiden"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HittTaksanTittanumar ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HittHalluwai ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 1-4 with Vs. I 26-37 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" ;
    skos:note "What the rite is said to do is stated in the operative speeches and not in a summary: let the tongues of these days be cut off, let the words of these days be cut off; be clean henceforth of mouth and tongue. The claim is about the words that were said and not about the disposition of the two persons, and nothing in the tablet says that they will not quarrel again."@en .

pmc:HittTarpalli a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The animal that stands as substitute for the heads and bodies of the pair"@en, "Das Tier als Ersatz für Köpfe und Körper des Paares"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "tarpalliš"@en ;
    skos:definition "The black sheep the Old Woman lifts over the two quarrellers and declares to be the substitute of their heads and of their whole bodies, with the curses in its mouth and on its tongue; the pair then spit into its mouth, and it is cut up, dismembered and burnt on the hearth with honey and oil poured over it."@en ;
    skos:note "The transfer is made by an act of the patients and not of the operator: the spitting is theirs. Three animals carry it in sequence in this version — a white sheep, then the black sheep, then a piglet — and the first two are both named substitute, so the substitution is not a single act but a graded series."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 47-55 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" .

pmc:HittSahturUlNepis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "What the piglet will not see again"@en, "Was das Ferkel nicht wiedersehen wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ground the Old Woman states for the piglet's efficacy: it has been fattened with grass and grain, and it will not see the sky again and will not see the other piglets again; so let the evil curses likewise not come to the two ritual patrons."@en ;
    skos:note "The analogy runs on a fact about the animal that the operator is about to make true, and the certainty of the premise is the whole of the argument. The same structure recurs twice in this tablet: the clay does not return to the clay pit and the cumin does not turn white and cannot be used for seed a second time. In each case what guarantees the claim is an irreversibility that the operator produces."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. I 56-Vs. II 2, with Vs. II 7-13 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" .

pmc:HittNakkussi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The scapegoat that is not killed and goes to the Old Woman"@en, "Der Sündenbock, der nicht getötet wird und der Alten Frau zufällt"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "nakkušši-"@en ;
    skos:definition "A further sheep is driven up and called the scapegoat; the Old Woman presents it to the Sun-god as a substitute in the pair's place, with mouth and tongue, breaks a sweet loaf and pours a libation of wine; and this sheep they do not kill, but the Old Woman herself takes it."@en ;
    skos:note "The practitioner's fee stands inside the operative text and is paid in the animal that carries the evil. It is the only one of the four animals of this rite that survives: the white sheep is buried, the black sheep is burnt, the piglet is buried, and the scapegoat is given to the Sun-god in word and to the operator in fact. The same tablet gives her the pair's fine garments a column later."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Vs. II 47'-53' with Rs. III 25 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" .

pmc:HittKisibApiyakku a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The seal to be opened only when the olden kings come back"@en, "Das Siegel, das erst zu öffnen ist, wenn die einstigen Könige wiederkommen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The last act of the rite: the water the pair have poured over their heads and rinsed their hands and eyes with is poured into an ox horn and sealed, and the Old Woman says that when the olden kings come back and take account of the custom of the land, only then is this seal to be opened."@en ;
    skos:note "The condition under which the rite could be undone is set at an event that the transmission nowhere expects. The clause is not a prohibition — it names a case in which the seal is to be opened — and that is what makes it worth entering: a flat prohibition would be a rule about conduct, whereas this is a stated condition of release which cannot be met, so the irreversibility is produced by the condition rather than asserted."@en ;
    skos:note "The restrictive particle is what carries it: the adverb of place is extended by a particle that limits it to that case, and both reachable translations of the tablet agree that something is restricted to that moment and disagree about everything else. The disagreement is at pmc:HittKisibApiyakkuDisputatum, and it is the reason this node states the clause and not a reading of it."@en ;
    skos:note "The same device, with the bundle of the rite deposited where nobody opens it again, stands in the ritual of Ḫantitaššu at pmc:HittEKisibUlKinuzi. There it is stated flatly and not as a condition; here it is a condition that does not fall due. The two are the Hittite pair for the claim in generalizations/counted-thresholds.ttl, and the counter-search that found the second is recorded at pmp:HittSealOpeningSearch."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "Maštigga ritual against domestic quarrel, CTH 404.1.II, KBo 2.3+ Rs. IV 7-13 (critical edition, exemplar A, hethiter.net/: CTH 404.1.II, Expl. A, textus 2012-05-07)" .

pmc:HittKisibApiyakkuDisputatum a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the olden kings come back and the seal is opened, or depart and the tablet is laid down"@en, "Ob die einstigen Könige wiederkommen und das Siegel geöffnet wird, oder fortgehen und die Tafel niedergelegt wird"@de ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:HittKisibApiyakku ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HittiteRitual ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Mastigga ;
    dcterms:source "F. Hrozný, Hethitische Keilschrifttexte aus Boghazköi. In Umschrift, mit Übersetzung und Kommentar, Boghazköi-Studien 3, Leipzig: J. C. Hinrichs 1919, p. 89, on KBo 2.3 Rs. IV 10-13 with his notes 7 to 9" ;
    dcterms:source "A. Goetze, Ritual against Domestic Quarrel, in J. B. Pritchard (ed.), Ancient Near Eastern Texts Relating to the Old Testament, 3rd ed., Princeton 1969, p. 351, on the same four lines" ;
    skos:note "Four lines, and the two translations differ at every joint. The editio princeps of 1919 renders the kings as going away rather than returning, the sealed object as a stone tablet rather than a seal, the verb as laying down rather than opening, and the restrictive adverb as there rather than only then; its author asks in a footnote whether the paragraph is urging the Hittite kings to spread these prescriptions abroad. The 1969 translation renders the kings as returning and examining the state of the land, and the clause as a seal to be broken then and then only. The later reading is the one the subsequent literature follows, and the earlier one is not a careless reading but a different construal of a text whose vocabulary was then new."@en ;
    skos:note "Why it is entered rather than settled by preferring the later translation. What this holding wants from the clause is whether the transmission set the undoing of its own rite beyond reach, and on the 1919 construal it did no such thing — the paragraph would then be a direction about the tablet and not about the rite's reversal. The claim at pmc:HittKisibApiyakku therefore rests on a reading, and a reader who takes the other one is entitled to strike it. That is a stronger statement of the holding's position than a node that reports only the received translation."@en ;
    skos:note "What the original wording settles and what it does not. The words for the kings, for the sealing of the horn in the preceding paragraph and for the restrictive particle are read the same way by both; the verb of motion, the designation of the sealed thing and the verb of the last line are what the two construe differently. A third treatment that would decide between them is in print."@en .
